# Line endings, pinned because a Windows checkout silently broke the demo.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so a fresh `git clone` rewrites every text
# file with CRLF. The documented Windows step `Copy-Item .env.example .env` then produced a `.env`
# whose every value ended in a carriage return, and `LLM_MODE=fixture\r` is not a member of
# `LLMMode` — so the API refused to start with a validation error naming the enum and never
# mentioning the invisible character. The file looked correct in every editor.
#
# Everything in this repository is consumed by Linux: the API, migrations, seed and both
# verification scripts run inside the container. `eol=lf` is therefore not a preference, it is what
# the runtime requires.
#
# `working-tree-encoding` is deliberately not set: the files are UTF-8 and must stay UTF-8 on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a CR is actively harmful rather than merely untidy.
# `.env.example` is the one that broke the demo; the rest are read by tools inside the image.
.env.example    text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.tsx           text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never touches the bytes.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
